{#
  picture(asset, sizes, options)

  Renders a <picture> with AVIF and WebP <source srcset>s across widths and a
  fallback <img> (original format). Width-only transforms → aspect ratio
  preserved, no crop. width/height on the <img> come from the transform to avoid
  layout shift.

  Source order is the selection order: the browser takes the first <source> whose
  type it understands, so AVIF sits above WebP and anything older falls through to
  the <img>. The AVIF source is omitted entirely on a host that can't encode it —
  see craft.frontend.supportsAvif() for why that's checked rather than assumed.

  An image can opt into a colour placeholder: a gradient between its two dominant
  colours, shown until the image itself has arrived on screen. It rides on custom
  properties rather than an element, so there's no extra markup and nothing to
  style around — see _picture.scss for the reveal, and
  FrontEndVariable::imageTones() for how the colours are picked and cached.

  options (all optional):
    widths   — list of srcset widths (default 320…1200)
    fallback — <img> width (default 640)
    loading  — 'lazy' (default) | 'eager'
    fetchpriority — 'high' to hint the resource is needed early. Omitted by default,
               and only ever right for an image that is genuinely in the first
               viewport: the hint works by taking priority FROM the other requests
               in flight, so spending it on something below the fold slows the
               fonts and CSS that decide first paint. Pairs with loading: 'eager'
               — a lazy image isn't requested early enough for a priority to
               matter. Applies to whichever <source> the browser picks.
    class    — class for the <img>
    tone     — true to show the colour placeholder. OFF by default, and opt-in
               rather than opt-out on purpose: it's a flourish for imagery the
               visitor has come to look at (the case study pages), and it reads as
               noise everywhere else — in a Jonson answer it turns a rail of
               supporting thumbnails into a row of flashing colour blocks while
               they're trying to read. Never worth it for an image that is mostly
               transparent either, where a colour block behind it reads as a
               background the image doesn't have.
    ratio    — width / height, e.g. 16/9: every transform is then a CROP to that
               shape, from the asset's focal point, at each width. Without it,
               transforms are width-only and the asset keeps its own shape.
    alt      — the <img>'s alt text, overriding the asset's own. Pass '' for a
               picture that's decorative in context (a thumbnail beside a linked
               title that already names the page) so a screen reader skips it.
    quality  — 1–100, for the WebP source and the <img> fallback ONLY. Omitted,
               Craft's defaultImageQuality (82) applies. Worth knowing that this now
               reaches very few visitors: almost everything current takes the AVIF,
               which is encoded at its own fixed quality and ignores this. Turning it
               up is a change to what old Safari and IE-era browsers get, not to what
               the image looks like for most people — the AVIF knobs are
               AVIF_QUALITY in FrontEndVariable and AVIF_CHROMA in the Images
               service.
#}
{% macro picture(asset, sizes, options) %}
    {% if asset %}
        {% set options = options ?? {} %}
        {% set widths = options.widths ?? [320, 480, 640, 960, 1200] %}
        {% set fallback = options.fallback ?? 640 %}
        {% set loading = options.loading ?? 'lazy' %}
        {% set fetchpriority = options.fetchpriority ?? null %}
        {% set klass = options.class ?? '' %}
        {% set sizes = sizes ?? '100vw' %}
        {# Merged into each transform rather than set as a key with a null value:
           passing `quality: null` to getUrl() would override the default with
           nothing, not fall back to it. #}
        {% set quality = options.quality is defined and options.quality ? options.quality : null %}
        {% set q = quality ? { quality: quality } : {} %}
        {# A ratio makes every transform a crop: height from the width. #}
        {% set ratio = options.ratio ?? null %}
        {% set alt = options.alt is defined ? options.alt : (asset.alt ?: asset.title) %}
        {# Null on an SVG, on a file Imagick can't read, or when switched off —
           `has-tone` is what the CSS keys the reveal off, so an image without one
           simply shows normally instead of fading in from nothing. #}
        {% set tone = (options.tone ?? false) ? craft.frontend.imageTones(asset) : null %}
        {# Deliberately ignores `quality`. AVIF is encoded at its own measured
           setting — see avifQuality() — because the two codecs don't share a scale
           and matching the numbers up only made AVIF worse. #}
        {% set avifQ = { quality: craft.frontend.avifQuality() } %}
        <picture class="c-picture{{ tone ? ' has-tone' }}"{% if tone %} style="--tone-a: {{ tone.a }}; --tone-b: {{ tone.b }}"{% endif %}>
            {% if craft.frontend.supportsAvif() %}
                <source
                    type="image/avif"
                    srcset="{% for w in widths %}{{ asset.getUrl({ width: w, format: 'avif' }|merge(ratio ? { height: (w / ratio)|round, mode: 'crop' } : {})|merge(avifQ)) }} {{ w }}w{{ not loop.last ? ', ' }}{% endfor %}"
                    sizes="{{ sizes }}">
            {% endif %}
            <source
                type="image/webp"
                srcset="{% for w in widths %}{{ asset.getUrl({ width: w, format: 'webp' }|merge(ratio ? { height: (w / ratio)|round, mode: 'crop' } : {})|merge(q)) }} {{ w }}w{{ not loop.last ? ', ' }}{% endfor %}"
                sizes="{{ sizes }}">
            <img
                class="c-picture__img{% if klass %} {{ klass }}{% endif %}"
                {% set fb = { width: fallback }|merge(ratio ? { height: (fallback / ratio)|round, mode: 'crop' } : {}) %}
                src="{{ asset.getUrl(fb|merge(q)) }}"
                width="{{ asset.getWidth(fb) }}"
                height="{{ asset.getHeight(fb) }}"
                {# The asset's own Alt Text field first. `title` is only a fallback and
                   a poor one: Craft auto-fills it from the filename, so an asset
                   nobody has retitled announces itself to a screen reader as
                   "6826106d8521545815a7cf5b Omidyar Network Case Study Asset8
                   Pattern". Fill in Alt Text on the asset. #}
                alt="{{ alt }}"
                loading="{{ loading }}"
                {%- if fetchpriority %} fetchpriority="{{ fetchpriority }}"{% endif %}
                decoding="async">
        </picture>
    {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
